---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Builder from '../../components/Builder.astro';
import DownloadButton from '../../components/DownloadButton.astro';
import RisingTent from '../../components/RisingTent.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';
const toc = [
  ['install', 'Install'],
  ['before', 'Before you start'],
  ['first-run', 'Your first village'],
  ['team', 'With a team'],
  ['feedback', 'Sending feedback'],
  ['updates', 'Updates'],
  ['limits', 'Known limits'],
];
---
<Base title="Beta guide" description="How to install the Clash of Claudes beta on your Mac, set up your first village, use it with a team, send bugs and ideas from inside the app, and what is still rough.">
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Clash of Claudes', item: 'https://clashofclaudes.crevn.xyz/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Beta guide', item: 'https://clashofclaudes.crevn.xyz/beta/' },
    ],
  })} />
  <section class="page-hero" aria-labelledby="page-title">
    <h1 class="title" id="page-title" data-split="lines" data-intro>Beta guide</h1>
    <p class="page-hero__lede">Everything you need to go from download to a working village in a few minutes, and how to tell us what broke.</p>
    <div class="page-hero__art drop-in" aria-hidden="true">
      <RisingTent agent="opencode" rise={0.45} building loading="eager" />
      <Builder sheet="craftsman-opencode" pose="hammer" />
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="doc">
    <nav class="doc__toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ol>{toc.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol>
    </nav>
    <div class="doc__body">
      <section id="install" aria-labelledby="install-h">
        <h2 id="install-h">Install</h2>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li><span class="steps__n" aria-hidden="true">1</span><span><strong>Download</strong> <code>Clash-of-Claudes.dmg</code>. It is one universal build for Apple Silicon and Intel.</span></li>
          <li><span class="steps__n" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span><strong>Open</strong> the disk image and drag Clash of Claudes onto Applications.</span></li>
          <li><span class="steps__n" aria-hidden="true">3</span><span><strong>Launch</strong> it from Applications or Spotlight.</span></li>
        </ol>
        <p>The app is signed with a Developer ID and notarized by Apple, so macOS opens it like any other downloaded app. The first launch may ask you to confirm that you want to open something from the internet. That is expected.</p>
        <DownloadButton />
        <p>Older builds are on the <a href={site.releases}>releases page</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="before" aria-labelledby="before-h">
        <h2 id="before-h">Before you start</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>{site.requirements}.</li>
          <li>At least one agent CLI installed and signed in: Claude Code (<code>claude</code>), Codex (<code>codex</code>), Antigravity CLI (<code>agy</code>), OpenCode (<code>opencode</code>) or Grok Build (<code>grok</code>). Any other command works as a custom builder.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="note">The app does not install agents, log in for you or manage API keys. Each agent uses the login you already set up for it. Open the Town Hall to see which CLIs were found, their versions, and a copyable install command for the missing ones.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="first-run" aria-labelledby="first-h">
        <h2 id="first-h">Your first village</h2>
        <p>The first launch asks what to call you; the Town Hall’s plate then carries your name. It also offers a two-minute tour on a sample compound that exists only in memory: you raise it, start a sample session, answer its permission prompt, review it and close it. No files change and no agents run. Skip it any time, or replay it from Settings.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Open workspace</strong> and pick a project folder. It becomes a walled compound around the Town Hall.</li>
          <li>Click the compound, then <strong>+ New session</strong>. Pick a builder, an agent, and optionally a model, effort and first task. The agent starts in a real terminal in that folder.</li>
          <li>Click any builder or tent for its live terminal in the side panel. Pop it out for a larger view, or use <strong>+ Terminal</strong> for a plain shell.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>macOS may ask whether Clash of Claudes can read your Downloads, Desktop or Documents folder. That is an agent or terminal inside the app asking, the same prompt any terminal app shows. Allow it for folders that hold your workspaces.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="team" aria-labelledby="team-h">
        <h2 id="team-h">With a team</h2>
        <p>Optional, and off until you turn it on. It needs a Vercel team, a recent <code>vercel</code> command (<code>npm i -g vercel@latest</code>) and <code>vercel login</code>. Everything here is in <strong>Settings › Team</strong>, which says what is missing.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Share a workspace.</strong> Turn on Vercel Board, then <strong>Share on the team board</strong> in a workspace’s panel. Its sessions show on your team’s board: who is working, waiting or done.</li>
          <li><strong>Invite people.</strong> Under People, invite a teammate by their Vercel email as a Visitor (looks), a Helper (also answers a builder’s questions) or Trusted (also allows and denies its tool calls). Your village is closed to everyone else.</li>
          <li><strong>Pair their Mac</strong> before a Helper or Trusted person can act: press <strong>Pair their Mac</strong>, send them the code, and confirm that both Macs show the same number.</li>
          <li><strong>Visit.</strong> Villages you were invited to appear at the top left. Click one to walk in, click a builder to see what it waits for, and press <strong>Home</strong> to come back.</li>
          <li><strong>Lend and borrow builders.</strong> Under Lent builders, choose who may hand a builder a task. To borrow, visit a teammate’s village, click one of their workspaces, pick a builder and write the task. It runs on their Mac and comes back as a branch on the repository’s <code>origin</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Answer from your phone.</strong> Under Phone remote, deploy the remote to your team once, then <strong>Pair a phone</strong> and scan the code. On an iPhone, add the page to the Home Screen first.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A lent builder is a teammate running an agent on your Mac with your logins, so lend only to people you would give that. The <a href={`${site.source}#team-vercel-board`}>README</a> has the full detail and the limits, and <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a> says what goes to the board.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="feedback" aria-labelledby="feedback-h">
        <h2 id="feedback-h">Sending feedback</h2>
        <p>Everything goes from inside the app, so we get the details that matter without you digging for them.</p>
        <ul class="menu-paths">
          <li><kbd>Help</kbd><span aria-label="then">›</span><kbd>Report a Bug…</kbd></li>
          <li><kbd>Help</kbd><span aria-label="then">›</span><kbd>Request a Feature…</kbd></li>
          <li><kbd>Settings</kbd><span aria-label="then">›</span><kbd>Beta feedback</kbd></li>
        </ul>
        <p>A report holds what you type, plus app and system details: app, macOS and agent versions, how many workspaces and sessions you have, and the last error the app showed. Never workspace names or tasks. A screenshot is attached only if you tick the box. You see all of it before it is sent.</p>
        <h3>What helps most</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Which agent the builder was running, and what it was doing.</li>
          <li>What you expected to see in the village, and what you saw instead.</li>
          <li>For Codex, OpenCode and Grok Build: whether working, needs you and finished matched what the terminal showed.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="updates" aria-labelledby="updates-h">
        <h2 id="updates-h">Updates</h2>
        <p>The app checks for new beta builds and updates itself, so you are always on the latest one. Apart from anonymous usage counts and a team board, both off until you turn them on, the check is the only network call it makes on its own, and you can turn it off in Settings (the gear at the top right, or <span class="keys"><kbd>⌘</kbd> <kbd>,</kbd></span>).</p>
      </section>

      <section id="limits" aria-labelledby="limits-h">
        <h2 id="limits-h">Known limits</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Mac only for now.</li>
          <li>Claude Code and Antigravity CLI were checked against real runs. Grok Build’s status reports were checked against a real grok, but not yet a full turn. Codex and OpenCode support was written from their documentation and still needs real-world confirmation. If one misbehaves, the terminal always works.</li>
          <li>Allow and deny from the village work for Claude Code and Antigravity CLI. For Codex, OpenCode and Grok Build, answer in the terminal.</li>
          <li>In sessions started from the app, Codex’s <code>notify</code> and <code>developer_instructions</code> settings are overridden on the command line. Your <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code> is never edited.</li>
          <li>Quitting stops your sessions (the app asks first). They come back paused. Resume continues Claude Code, Codex, Antigravity and Grok Build on their own conversation; OpenCode and custom commands start fresh in the same folder.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
